---

<ContentLayout
  locale={locale}
  route="guides/more-reviews"
  title="How to Get More Google Reviews | Wiserrise"
  description="How to get more Google reviews within the rules: the right moment, a short review link, writing replies. And why buying reviews and incentives are banned."
  eyebrow="GUIDE · REVIEWS"
  h1="How do you get more Google reviews?"
  crumb="Getting more reviews"
  lede="The way to get more Google reviews is to ask your customers regularly, one at a time, the moment the job is done, with a link that opens in one tap. Buying reviews, collecting them in exchange for discounts and sending only satisfied customers to Google all break the rules. Set your target based on your competitors and spread the flow over weeks."
  schemaType="article"
  datePublished="2026-08-01"
  dateModified="2026-11-06"
  breadcrumb={[
    { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
    { label: 'Guides', href: p('guides') },
  ]}
  features={features}
  toc={toc}
  faq={faq}
  related={[
    { label: 'Ranking higher on Google Maps', href: p('guides/rank-higher'), text: 'Where reviews fit in rankings and the order to work in.' },
    { label: 'Business not showing on Google', href: p('guides/not-showing'), text: 'If you have enough reviews and are still missing from the map, rule out the causes one by one.' },
    { label: 'Google Business Profile Management', href: p('services/gbp'), text: 'We manage your category, service structure, reviews and posts together.' },
    { label: 'SEO for lawyers', href: p('industries/law'), text: 'What’s possible on the profile and review side within the advertising rules for lawyers.' },
  ]}
  relatedHeading="Related pages"
  ctaLabel="Audit your profile for free"
  ctaHref={p('tools/gbp-audit')}
>
  <h2 id="hedef">How many reviews are enough?</h2>

  <p>
    There’s no number that applies to everyone. Your benchmark is the review volume of the businesses in the top three on
    the map for your target search. In some industries that means 20 reviews, in others 200.
  </p>

  <p>
    It’s easy to check. Type your search into Google Maps and note the review count and rating of the top three. If
    you’re clearly below them, reviews are the priority.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you’re level with your competitors and still invisible, the problem is somewhere else, most likely in the
    category. In that case, start with the checks in the <a href={p('guides/not-showing')}>business not showing on
    Google</a> guide.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>Volume comes before the average rating.</strong> 80 reviews at 4.7 make a stronger profile than 6 reviews at
      5.0. Don’t chase a perfect score; a few 4-star reviews make your profile more believable.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="an">When should you ask for a review?</h2>

  <p>
    Right after the job is done, while the experience is fresh. Timing decides your review rate more than the wording does.
  </p>

  <p>
    Say you run a vehicle inspection business in Perth. The customer is holding the report, has just learned that the
    car they’re about to buy came out clean, and is relieved. This is exactly the moment to ask, before they leave the
    premises.
  </p>

  <p>The right moment depends on the industry:</p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Industry</th>
          <th>The right moment</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Vehicle inspection</td>
          <td>When handing over the report, while the customer is still on site</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Dental clinic</td>
          <td>When treatment is finished and the follow-up appointment has passed</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Physiotherapy</td>
          <td>When the session program ends</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Law</td>
          <td>When the matter is closed, one client at a time; read the note on professional rules below</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Hairdresser, repair service, retail</td>
          <td>Within a few hours after the service or delivery is complete</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Few customers respond to reminders sent days later. They remember the experience, but the moment has passed, so
    they don’t act.
  </p>

  <h2 id="nasil">How do you ask for a review?</h2>

  <p>Make writing a review as easy as possible for the customer. This is the routine we set up:</p>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Get the short review link.</strong> Your business profile’s management screen has a review link. It takes
      the customer straight to the review screen, so they don’t have to search for you on the map.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Ask face to face and send the link right away.</strong> Say it in one sentence when you hand over the work,
      and send the link by text message or WhatsApp at that moment. Sending a message alone brings fewer responses.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Keep a QR code on site.</strong> Put the link’s QR code at the checkout, in the waiting room or on the
      handover desk.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Ask once.</strong> Persistent reminders spoil the customer’s mood.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    There’s a clear line between giving direction and giving text. Saying “We’d love it if you mentioned the service you
    had and which neighborhood you came from” is fine, because the customer writes the sentences. The moment you hand
    over ready-made text, the reviews start to look alike and get caught by the filter.
  </p>

  <h2 id="kurallar">Which review tactics does Google ban?</h2>

  <p>
    Let’s be clear about this: don’t do any of the following, even if your competitor does. They raise the count in the
    short term, then put your profile at risk.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Buying reviews.</strong> A review written for money is a fake review and breaks Google’s rules. If the filter
      catches them, the reviews are removed and the profile risks suspension.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Collecting reviews with incentives.</strong> A discount, a gift, a prize draw or “coffee’s on us if you leave
      a review” all lead to the same place. Reviews that get caught are removed, and a repeat can get the profile
      suspended.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sending the link only to happy customers.</strong> Asking “are you satisfied?” first and directing only the
      ones who say yes to Google is explicitly banned. The industry term for it is review gating.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    If your competitor is ahead with bought reviews, report the violation to Google and build your own genuine flow.
    Their reviews can be removed at any moment, while your real reviews keep adding up.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>A note for lawyers and healthcare businesses:</strong> Professional rules come on top of Google’s, and
      they differ by country and state. Some US states, for example, have rules on how lawyers ask clients for reviews,
      and in Australia AHPRA restricts testimonials in health advertising. Check the rules with your own bar, law society
      or health regulator before you start. In law, ask when
      each matter is closed, with no ready-made review text. Wherever you ask, ask one person at a time, without
      incentives, without picking and in a single sentence; the details are on our <a href={p('industries/law')}>lawyers</a>
      and <a href={p('industries/dental')}>dentists</a> pages.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="filtre">Why are your reviews disappearing?</h2>

  <p>
    Google’s filter quietly removes reviews it finds suspicious. No notification comes, the count just drops. Typical
    patterns that get caught:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>A sudden pile.</strong> Lots of reviews arriving on a new profile within a few days.</li>
    <li><strong>Reviews from the same network.</strong> Having customers write reviews in bulk while they’re on the business’s Wi-Fi.</li>
    <li><strong>Accounts with no history.</strong> Accounts that have never reviewed anything before, with a single review each.</li>
    <li><strong>Texts that resemble each other.</strong> The same template repeated with small changes.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The best-known trap is the bulk request. Messaging your list of past customers on the same day is technically allowed,
    but a pile of reviews arriving within a few days is exactly the pattern the filter looks for.
  </p>

  <p>
    Spread the list over weeks. Two or three real reviews a week is a safer flow than twenty reviews collected in one
    week.
  </p>

  <h2 id="yanit">How should you reply to reviews?</h2>

  <p>
    Briefly and in a human voice, to the good ones and the bad ones. Replying to reviews is also among Google’s own
    recommendations for local rankings. A reply shows the next customer researching you that the business is active.
  </p>

  <p>
    Template replies like “Your lovely review brightened our day, seeing our brand shine on the digital stage is an
    indescribable joy” give themselves away in the first sentence. Readers can tell you don’t really read your reviews.
  </p>

  <p>
    Mention the service they received plainly, say thank you, and stop. Two lines like “Thank you, Sarah, we’re glad the
    report put your mind at ease, enjoy your new car” are enough.
  </p>

  <p>
    Healthcare and law are an exception: don’t name the service in your reply. A reply like “We’re glad you were happy
    with your implant treatment” discloses that person’s treatment to everyone.
  </p>

  <h2 id="olumsuz">What should you do if a negative review comes in?</h2>

  <p>Go step by step:</p>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Reach out privately first.</strong> Solve the problem by talking it through. If it’s solved, the customer can
      update the review themselves.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Then write a short, non-defensive reply.</strong> The person who will really read this reply is the next
      customer researching you. Don’t call the customer a liar and don’t go into detail.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>If it breaks the rules, report it.</strong> Insults, irrelevant content or reviews that clearly come from a
      competitor can be removed. The process isn’t guaranteed and can take weeks.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    Your strongest defense against a single negative review is your genuine review volume. On a profile with 120 reviews,
    one bad review gets lost; on a profile with 8, it’s the first thing people notice.
  </p>

